WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:02.668
-Our next guest
is a Pulitzer Prize-winning

00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:04.070
and Tony-nominated playwright

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.239
whose critically acclaimed
new musical "A Strange Loop"

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:08.808
is currently on Broadway
at the Lyceum Theater.

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:11.745
Fresh off the musical's
11 Tony nominations,

00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:14.847
please welcome to the show,
Michael R. Jackson, everybody.

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:17.483
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:24.891 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:24.891 --> 00:00:32.298 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:35.367
How are you, Michael?
-I'm doing pretty great, Seth.

00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:37.436
-You've already won a Pulitzer.

00:00:37.436 --> 00:00:40.440
You are now nominated
11 times for the Tony Award.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.975
Is it sinking in exactly
how people have responded

00:00:42.975 --> 00:00:44.210
to your show?

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:45.411
-It's sinking.

00:00:45.411 --> 00:00:47.246
I don't know if
it's quite gotten there yet,

00:00:47.246 --> 00:00:48.380
but it's there.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.349
-It is really, really exciting.

00:00:50.349 --> 00:00:52.084
And I think one of the things
that's coolest about this show

00:00:52.084 --> 00:00:55.954
is when people comment on it,
they stress how unique it is.

00:00:55.954 --> 00:00:59.458
Were you setting out to make
a show that was unique,

00:00:59.458 --> 00:01:02.294
compared to what we tend
to find on Broadway?

00:01:02.294 --> 00:01:04.697
-I mean, when I first set out
to work on this show,

00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:06.065
I was 23 years old.

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:09.402
I was just writing, like,
a sort of life raft for myself.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:11.870
So I don't -- and I definitely
wanted it to be,

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:14.406
you know, specific
and and nuanced and complex,

00:01:14.406 --> 00:01:16.242
but I had no real thoughts

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:18.344
about what anybody else
would think about it.

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:21.346
-So did that put less pressure
on you, because you weren't

00:01:21.346 --> 00:01:23.015
setting out to, you know,

00:01:23.015 --> 00:01:25.985
make this thing that would
take the world by storm?

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:29.321
-Yeah, because like back then,
no one cared who I was.

00:01:29.321 --> 00:01:30.723
[ Laughter ] Like, at all.

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:32.558
So I was like,
I'll just do whatever I want.

00:01:32.558 --> 00:01:34.127
-And you've referred to it

00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:37.663
as not autobiographical,
but self-referential.

00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:39.098
What is the distinction there?

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:42.068
-For me, autobiography
sort of suggests

00:01:42.068 --> 00:01:45.170
that there's a 1-to-1 ratio
of events to my life,

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.940
and I would say it's more
emotionally autobiographical.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:50.543
I felt everything that
the protagonist feels,

00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:53.145
but we have
different life experiences

00:01:53.145 --> 00:01:54.847
and the details are different.

00:01:54.847 --> 00:01:57.784
-So it is about an usher
named Usher?

00:01:57.784 --> 00:01:59.652
-Mm-hmm.
-You were a Broadway usher.

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:00.786
-I was, indeed.

00:02:00.786 --> 00:02:02.922
-Do you feel like
that was a skill,

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:04.490
or an experience, I should say,

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.125
that made you more skilled
in trying to write

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:07.627
a musical like this?

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:09.896
-Yes, because I didn't
want to usher anymore,

00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:12.398
so I needed to write
something that could, like,

00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:13.699
propel me out of that life.

00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:15.435
-Yeah, but would you --

00:02:15.435 --> 00:02:16.535
I mean, did you get a chance

00:02:16.535 --> 00:02:19.038
to see
show after show after show?

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:21.441
-Oh, I saw more
than show after show after show.

00:02:21.441 --> 00:02:23.276
I mean, I was like
constantly at the back,

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:25.344
you know, at the back
of the house of "The Lion King"

00:02:25.344 --> 00:02:27.179
night after night,
watching the animals,

00:02:27.179 --> 00:02:28.514
watching the animals.

00:02:28.514 --> 00:02:31.550
Watching, you know,
the people down the aisles.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:34.520
-Usher in the show names names.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.389
There are some real people
that are commented on.

00:02:37.389 --> 00:02:39.024
-For sure.
-Did you think

00:02:39.024 --> 00:02:40.426
in the writing, ever,

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:44.163
"Oh, I might have to
take this out one day if --"

00:02:44.163 --> 00:02:45.631
-I did, and then, like --

00:02:45.631 --> 00:02:48.300
there was this one draft
where I think I changed

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.903
the name to like,
"Skyler Terry."

00:02:50.903 --> 00:02:54.206
And I was like,
and then my director was like,

00:02:54.206 --> 00:02:55.374
"Get the hell out of here.
You're not doing that."

00:02:55.374 --> 00:02:56.809
So we left it the same.

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:58.511
And then ever since then, I felt
like I had full permission.

00:02:58.511 --> 00:02:59.845
-That's very nice.

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:03.583
So the song tonight
is called "Inner White Girl."

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:06.619
And so, again, this is
self-referential.

00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:08.053
This is something
that is true of you.

00:03:08.053 --> 00:03:10.189
Can you speak a little bit
about what this song is about?

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:12.825
-Yeah, I sort of
as a teenager, was like,

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:15.027
as angsty as you could get.

00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:17.397
And I was like, just coming out
and, like, feeling,

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:20.099
you know, sad and emo
all the time.

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:23.769
And of course, into Tori Amos
via my cousin who, like,

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:24.971
gave me her first two albums,

00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:26.505
"Little Earthquakes"
and "Under the Pink."

00:03:26.505 --> 00:03:29.108
And I sort of one night
turned all the lights off,

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:31.010
put my headphones on,
get under the covers,

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.745
and then Tori Amos
starts singing to me,

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:34.380
and I was a goner.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.848
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.848 --> 00:03:37.616
-We were also talking
backstage about

00:03:37.616 --> 00:03:40.219
our shared love affair
for Liz Phair as well.

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:42.855
-Yeah, Liz Phair
was in my twenties,

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:45.524
when I was just feeling,
like, even more emo,

00:03:45.524 --> 00:03:46.826
but a little more
punk rock about it.

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:47.994
-Yeah.

00:03:47.994 --> 00:03:49.929
-And, like, wandering around
New York City,

00:03:49.929 --> 00:03:52.031
just like, hating everything
in my life and everyone.

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:54.700
And Liz is really
good music to have for you,

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:55.701
for that and on that.

00:03:55.701 --> 00:03:56.735
-Yeah, I agree.

00:03:56.735 --> 00:03:58.771
I think she's
the closest thing I have

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.039
to an inner white girl,
is Liz Phair.

00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:02.075
That is the most I've
listened to on headphones.

00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:04.076
I feel like she makes you
feel very cool

00:04:04.076 --> 00:04:05.210
for hating everything.

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:07.646
-She does, because she has
such a, like,

00:04:07.646 --> 00:04:10.850
a really badass sense of humor
and a lot of candor

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.719
as she sort of speaks
about her experiences

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:14.654
through the characters
in her songs.

00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:16.489
-This is also very exciting.

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:18.023
It's no Pulitzer, mind you,

00:04:18.023 --> 00:04:21.327
but you saw Tori Amos
in concert last night.

00:04:21.327 --> 00:04:22.461
-Yes.

00:04:22.461 --> 00:04:24.363
-Tonight you
are going again, backstage.

00:04:24.363 --> 00:04:25.565
You're going to meet her
for the first time.

00:04:25.565 --> 00:04:26.799
-I'm gonna meet her tonight.

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:28.901
-How do you think
it's going to go?

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:30.269
What are you worried about
or excited about?

00:04:30.269 --> 00:04:33.873
-I'm worried that I might,
you know, cry all over...

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:34.974
[ Laughter ]

00:04:34.974 --> 00:04:36.108
...like, everything
that she's wearing

00:04:36.108 --> 00:04:38.878
and make a complete
imbecile of myself.

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:40.646
-What are her expectations?

00:04:40.646 --> 00:04:42.615
Is she aware of what a big part
of the show she is

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:44.350
and a big part of your story?

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:45.584
-I don't think so.

00:04:45.584 --> 00:04:47.686
I think I've kept
that pretty close to the vest,

00:04:47.686 --> 00:04:50.923
because then she might not
let me come back and see her.

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:52.592
[ Laughter ]

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:55.294
-Now, after the success
of the show,

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:56.763
it must be so exciting.

00:04:56.763 --> 00:04:58.898
It was this Monday when you find
out about all the nominations.

00:04:58.898 --> 00:05:00.099
Like, do you now have --

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:02.735
what is your anticipation
leading up to the Tonys?

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:04.603
Is it mostly just excitement?

00:05:04.603 --> 00:05:06.739
Do you think you'll get nervous
as it gets closer?

00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:08.107
-I'll definitely get nervous,

00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:11.444
but it's excitement, for me,
because, like,

00:05:11.444 --> 00:05:15.314
I get to celebrate so many
of the people in my company.

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:16.582
And that, for me,
in a lot of ways,

00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:19.185
was way more exciting
than any nomination I got,

00:05:19.185 --> 00:05:21.353
because I've been working
with a lot of these people

00:05:21.353 --> 00:05:25.991
in the cast for up to 15 years,
and on this show.

00:05:25.991 --> 00:05:28.360
And so, getting to
celebrate them,

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:29.962
for me, is going to be,
like, the best part.

00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:31.697
-So this is, I mean,
I think this is really --

00:05:31.697 --> 00:05:34.066
because I think people
might read about this show

00:05:34.066 --> 00:05:36.035
and hear about this show
and think that this was, like,

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:38.437
an overnight success.
-Oh, yeah. So overnight.

00:05:38.437 --> 00:05:40.706
-I think that's a really
important thing for people.

00:05:40.706 --> 00:05:44.576
-Yeah, George W. Bush had like,
just been re-elected --

00:05:44.576 --> 00:05:47.413
re-elected or elected, when
I was first working on this.

00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:49.848
-Yeah, so sometimes
I think it's a good --

00:05:49.848 --> 00:05:51.684
I don't know, a good reminder
that sometimes you have to

00:05:51.684 --> 00:05:53.986
put your nose to the grindstone
and push through a lot of stuff.

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:55.688
-Yeah, I think, like, for me,

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:58.691
the thing that I learned
about making art

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:02.861
in working on this piece is that
it's worth it to take your time.

00:06:02.861 --> 00:06:05.364
And that's something I always
tell, like, young students

00:06:05.364 --> 00:06:07.567
when I talk to them,
and they're ready

00:06:07.567 --> 00:06:09.869
to, like, put their work on an
assembly line and for it to,

00:06:09.869 --> 00:06:13.239
you know, chart number one
2 minutes later,

00:06:13.239 --> 00:06:15.507
I'm like, "No, I spent 18 years
working on a musical,

00:06:15.507 --> 00:06:17.443
so good luck."

00:06:17.443 --> 00:06:18.511
[ Laughter ]

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:19.712
-Do you think
if you looked back --

00:06:19.712 --> 00:06:20.880
I mean, I'm sure you
still have it,

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.750
but how massively different
is what it is now

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:27.119
versus the first time
you put it down on the page?

00:06:27.119 --> 00:06:29.822
-I mean, it's different
in the sense that,

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:32.058
like, when I started it,
it was a monologue,

00:06:32.058 --> 00:06:33.592
and now it's a musical.

00:06:33.592 --> 00:06:36.462
But, like, the core ideas
and the feelings that

00:06:36.462 --> 00:06:39.131
I was trying to capture
are the same.

00:06:39.131 --> 00:06:41.733
So I've been sort of really
trying to track that

00:06:41.733 --> 00:06:43.002
all the way from the beginning.

00:06:43.002 --> 00:06:44.669
-Well, I'm very excited
that we get to see

00:06:44.669 --> 00:06:45.771
a performance tonight.

00:06:45.771 --> 00:06:46.739
And thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:48.674
Congratulations on everything.

00:06:48.674 --> 00:06:50.209
-Michael R. Jackson, everybody.

00:06:50.209 --> 00:06:52.244
"A Strange Loop" is
playing at the Lyceum Theater,

00:06:52.244 --> 00:06:53.812
and you can now stream tracks

00:06:53.812 --> 00:06:55.081
from the Broadway
cast recording.

